  "reconstruction": {
    "candidateId": "templo_mayor",
    "constructionStatus": "authored-medium-fi-surviving-excavation",
    "scope": "Templo Mayor surviving main-pyramid excavation: nested low volcanic-stone construction-stage traces, interrupted terraces and west-facing stairs, the paired older Tláloc/Huitzilopochtli shrine bases under their modern protective canopy, two small mapped covers, pale plaster remnants, low walls and a supported visitor crossing. The main mapped below-grade footprint is an excavation, not a tall intact pyramid. Casa de las Águilas, the museum, cathedral and independent neighboring buildings remain separate. No hypothetical 1521 temple towers or copied narrative sculptures are reconstructed.",
    "identityBasis": "Q774021/way1423783282 identifies the main-pyramid footprint. Four pinned standard PMTiles records control that below-street outline and three protective covers. Named map points fix the older Tláloc shrine to the north and Huitzilopochtli shrine to the south. The ordinary west-side picture establishes west-facing stair runs; the mapped outline/canopy edges give an approximately 5.85-degree east/south construction axis. Raw +X east,+Z south at heading 0; component positions beyond these controls are proportional visualization estimates.",
    "heightBasis": "The photographs show surviving low terraces and damaged stairs, broad exposed floors and modern covers rather than an intact ancient pyramid. Construction traces rise 0.65–3.1m above the authored excavation floor; older shrine bases are 3.8m above it with short surviving walls. The main canopy eaves/ridge are 8.4/9.6m above that floor. Individual step counts, plaster patches, wall breaks, canopy joinery and artifact positions are estimates, not surveyed archaeological phases.",
    "terrainBasis": "The original 161-square/6m standard Mapzen grid is retained unchanged. A bounded opening follows only the mapped layer−1 main-pyramid footprint. An opaque authored floor seals the entire pocket at every LOD, conservatively below the sampled coarse grade; retaining sides rise to the native boundary. This is a visualization datum, not an excavation survey. Surrounding terrain, native water, independent buildings and the mapped pedestrian alignment remain intact. The authored supported crossing follows that pedestrian alignment above the pocket.",
